# Hypercolor device permissions
# Install: just udev-install
# Grants access to the physically logged-in user via systemd-logind (TAG+="uaccess").
# Some hidraw nodes can miss their ACL replay on existing devices, so we also set a
# conservative fallback group/mode for the supported vendor families.
# A replug or reboot is required after installation for existing devices.
#
# hidraw rules name the products Hypercolor registers, never a whole vendor,
# and on keyboards, mice, and headsets they grant only the HID interface
# Hypercolor opens, since the other interfaces carry keystrokes and pointer
# input. A new product ID needs its own hidraw rule here; the tests in
# crates/hypercolor-hal/tests/udev_rules_tests.rs fail until it has one.
# SMBus access is granted generically via `/dev/i2c-*` because bus nodes are
# host-controller devices, not vendor-scoped endpoints.
#
# hidraw/tty rules use ATTRS (walk up to parent USB device for vendor match).
# usb rules use ATTR + DEVTYPE filter to match the /dev/bus/usb/ device node directly.

# ─── Razer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Scoped per product and lighting interface, never vendor-wide. Razer
# keyboards, mice, and headsets expose keystroke, pointer, and media-key
# collections as hidraw nodes beside the lighting one, so a vendor-wide grant
# hands every member of the fallback group a keystroke feed.
#
# Each rule matches the USB interface's modalias, which carries the vendor,
# product, and interface number on one parent, so one line pins all three.
# A modalias match also never reaches a device plugged in behind a hub,
# which an ATTRS{idVendor} match does. None of these products gets a
# usb_device grant: Hypercolor reaches them through hidraw only, and write
# access to the USB device node is enough to detach usbhid from a keyboard.
#
# Interface 0: mice, mousepads, and accessories. On a mouse the lighting
# reports ride the mouse collection, so the grant also exposes pointer
# motion and buttons, never keystrokes; the keyboard collections mice use
# for macros sit on other interfaces and stay root-only.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{modalias}=="usb:v1532p0044d*ic03isc*ip*in00", MODE="0660", GROUP="users", TAG+="uaccess"
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{modalias}=="usb:v1532p0045d*ic03isc*ip*in00", MODE="0660", GROUP="users", TAG+="uaccess"
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{modalias}=="usb:v1532p0046d*ic03isc*ip*in00", MODE="0660", GROUP="users", TAG+="uaccess"
